ine 11: month not in list, day>30 False. Line 13: month != 2, day>29 False. So \"01-30-2023\" \u2192 True.\n\nFor day=31: line 11: day > 30 \u2192 True \u2192 returns False. So any day=31 returns False (bug).\n\nFor day=0, month=1: line 9: month in list and day<1 \u2192 True \u2192 False.\nFor day=0, month=4: line 9: month not in list; day > 31? No. Line 11: month in list and day<1 \u2192 True \u2192 False.\nFor day=0, month=2: line 9: no. Line 11: no. Line 13: month==2 and day<1 \u2192 True \u2192 False.\nFor day=0, month=... hmm, what if month is not in any of those lists and day < 1? All months 1-12 are covered: 1,3,5,7,8,10,12 in first list; 4,6,9,11 in second; 2 in third. So day<1 always returns False for valid months.\n\nNegative day like day=-5: day<1 \u2192 False via respective list. Good.\n\nDay=32: line 9: day > 31 \u2192 False. Even for month=2.\n\nSo the \"valid\" returning True cases: day in 1..29 (any month), or day=30 (any month except... line 11: day>30 false; line 13: day>29 false). So day=30 works for any month 1-12. Day=29 for any month (including February, though Feb 29 in non-leap year is wrong \u2014 the function doesn't check leap years).\n\nLet me pick tests:\n\n1. Test valid dates: \"01-15-2023\" \u2192 True, \"02-29-2020\" \u2192 True (note: function returns True even for Feb 29; also \"04-30-2021\" \u2192 True? day=30, month=4: line 11: (4 in list and day<1) \u2192 False; day>30 \u2192 False. Line 13: month != 2. So True.)\n\n2. Test month out of range: \"13-01-2023\" \u2192 False, \"00-01-2023\" \u2192 False.\n\n3. Test invalid days for 31-day months: \"01-00-2023\" \u2192 False (day < 1). \"01-32-2023\" \u2192 False (day > 31 via line 9).\n\n4. Test invalid days for 30-day months: \"04-00-2023\" \u2192 False, \"04-31-2023\" \u2192 False (day > 30 via line 11 \u2014 wait, line 11: (4 in [4,6,9,11] and day<1) or day>31... no, line 11 is `month in [4,6,9,11] and day < 1 or day > 30`. So day=31 > 30 \u2192 True \u2192 False. Good.)\n\n5. Test February: \"02-30-2023\" \u2192 False (day > 29). \"02-00-2023\" \u2192 False (month==2 and day<1). \"02-29-2020\" \u2192 True (day=29, not > 29; month==2 and day<1 \u2192 day=29 not < 1 \u2192 False \u2192 doesn't return False \u2192 returns True).\n\n6. Exception cases: \n   - \"1-1-2023\" with only 2 parts \u2192 ValueError on unpacking \u2192 False.\n   - \"01/15/2023\" \u2192 split('-') gives one element \u2192 ValueError \u2192 False.\n   - \"aa-15-2023\" \u2192 int('aa') raises ValueError \u2192 False.\n   - None input \u2192 AttributeError on strip \u2192 False. But wait, is None passed? date.strip() raises AttributeError \u2192 caught by except \u2192 return False. Good.\n   - Extra whitespace: \" 01-15-2023 \" \u2192 strip \u2192 works \u2192 True.\n\nBranch coverage: I need to cover:\n- Line 3: try entry \u2014 all tests.\n- Line 4: date.strip() \u2014 all.\n- Line 5: split with 3 parts \u2014 most; exception path with wrong number of parts.\n- Line 6: int conversions \u2014 most; exception with non-numeric.\n- Line 7: month<1 or month>12 \u2014 test month=0 and month=13, also month in range (both branches of the `or`).\n- Line 8: return False.\n- Line 9: month in list and day<1; day>31; also cases where neither.\n- Line 10: return False.\n- Line 11: month in [4,6,9,11] and day<1; day>30; neither.\n- Line 12: return False.\n- Line 13: month==2 and day<1; day>29; neither.\n- Line 14: return False.\n- Line 15-16: except \u2192 return False.\n- Line 18: return True.\n\nFor branch coverage of `month < 1 or month > 12`: month=0 covers first; month=13 covers second. Both return False at line 8.\n\nFor line 9 branches: \n- True branch: month=1, day=0 \u2192 False.\n- day>31 branch: month=1, day=32 \u2192 False. Also month=2, day=32 (month not in list, day>31) \u2192 False.\n- Neither: month=1, day=15.\n\nFor line 11:\n- month in list and day<1: month=4, day=0 \u2192 False.\n- day>30: month=1, day=31 \u2192 False (this is the buggy case). Also month=1, day=31.\n- Neither: month=4, day=15.\n\nFor line 13:\n- month==2 and day<1: month=2, day=0 \u2192 False.\n- day>29: month=2, day=30 \u2192 False.\n- Neither: month=2, day=15 \u2192 True.\n\nExceptions:\n- unpack error: \"1-2023\" \u2192 ValueError.\n- non-numeric: \"a-b-c\" \u2192 ValueError.\n- None \u2192 AttributeError.\n- Also maybe \"01-01-2023-extra\" \u2192 too many values \u2192 ValueError.\n\nWhitespace: \"  01-15-2023  \" \u2192 True (covers strip doing something).\n\nNow about imports: the function is in f124/__init__.py, so `from f124 import valid_date`.\n\nNo state pollution concerns since the function is pure \u2014 but the instructions say to be careful. No monkeypatching needed really, since no file I/O or global state. I'll just write pure tests.\n\nLet me write the test file:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom f124 import valid_date\n\n\ndef test_valid_dates_return_true():\n    # days 1..30 with any month are accepted by this implementation\n    assert valid_date(\"01-15-2023\") is True\n    assert valid_date(\"02-29-2020\") is True\n    assert valid_date(\"04-30-2021\") is True\n    assert valid_date(\"12-01-1999\") is True\n\n\ndef test_valid_date_strips_whitespace():\n    assert valid_date(\"  01-15-2023  \") is True\n\n\ndef test_month_out_of_range_returns_false():\n    assert valid_date(\"00-15-2023\") is False  # month < 1\n    assert valid_date(\"13-15-2023\") is False  # month > 12\n\n\ndef test_31_day_month_invalid_days():\n    assert valid_date(\"01-00-2023\") is False  # day < 1\n    assert valid_date(\"01-32-2023\") is False  # day > 31\n    assert valid_date(\"03-32-2023\") is False\n\n\ndef test_30_day_month_invalid_days():\n    assert valid_date(\"04-00-2023\") is False  # day < 1\n    assert valid_date(\"04-31-2023\") is False  # day > 30\n    assert valid_date(\"06-31-2023\") is False\n\n\ndef test_february_invalid_days():\n    assert valid_date(\"02-00-2023\") is False  # day < 1\n    assert valid_date(\"02-30-2023\") is False  # day > 29\n    assert valid_date(\"02-29-2020\") is True   # boundary accepted\n\n\ndef test_malformed_date_returns_false():\n    assert valid_date(\"01-2023\") is False        # too few parts\n    assert valid_date(\"01-15-2023-extra\") is False  # too many parts\n    assert valid_date(\"01/15/2023\") is False     # wrong separator\n\n\ndef test_non_numeric_parts_return_false():\n    assert valid_date(\"aa-bb-cccc\") is False\n    assert valid_date(\"1-b-2023\") is False\n\n\ndef test_none_input_returns_false():\n    assert valid_date(None) is False\n```\n\nWait \u2014 need to double check \"01-15-2023\": month=1, day=15, year=2023. Line 7: 1 >= 1, 1 <= 12 \u2192 no return. Line 9: (1 in [1,3,...] and 15 < 1) \u2192 False; 15 > 31 \u2192 False \u2192 no return. Line 11: (1 in [4,6,9,11]) \u2192 False; 15 > 30 \u2192 False \u2192 no return. Line 13: (1 == 2) \u2192 False; 15 > 29 \u2192 False \u2192 no return. Returns True. \u2713\n\n\"04-30-2021\": month=4, day=30. Line 9: 4 not in first list \u2192 (False and ...) False; 30 > 31 False. Line 11: 4 in [4,6,9,11] and 30 < 1 \u2192 False; 30 > 30 \u2192 False. Line 13: 4 != 2; 30 > 29 \u2192 True \u2192 return False! \n\nOops! Line 13: `if month == 2 and day < 1 or day > 29:` \u2014 day=30 > 29 \u2192 True \u2192 return False. So \"04-30-2021\" returns False due to the precedence bug! Let me recheck.\n\nLine 13 parses as `(month == 2 and day < 1) or (day > 29)`. day=30 > 29 \u2192 True \u2192 returns False. So day=30 is rejected for ALL months. Hmm, wait: line 11 also has `day > 30` \u2014 day=30 is not > 30, so line 11 doesn't trigger. But line 13 does because day=30 > 29.\n\nSo what's the max valid day? day must not be > 29 (from line 13) and not > 30 (line 11) and not > 31 (line 9). So day \u2264 29 for everything. Day 1-29 with any month 1-12 \u2192 True.\n\nLet me redo: \"04-30-2021\" \u2192 False. I need to fix my tests.\n\nValid dates: day in 1..29, month 1..12, year any int. E.g., \"01-15-2023\" \u2192 True, \"04-29-2021\" \u2192 True (day=29: line 13: (4==2 and 29<1) False; 29>29 False \u2192 OK). \"02-29-2020\" \u2192 True: line 13: (True and 29<1)=False; 29>29=False \u2192 OK \u2192 True.\n\nSo test valid: \"01-15-2023\", \"04-29-2021\", \"02-29-2020\", \"12-01-1999\", \"11-28-2022\".\n\nAnd for day=30: \"04-30-2021\" \u2192 False (via line 13's day>29). Actually that also exercises line 11's day>30? No \u2014 line 11 check: 30 > 30 is False, so line 11 doesn't return. Line 13 returns. Hmm, but I want to cover line 11's `day > 30` branch specifically: need day=31 with any month: \"04-31-2023\": line 9: 4 not in list, 31 > 31 False \u2192 pass. Line 11: (4 in list and 31<1) False; 31 > 30 \u2192 True \u2192 return False at line 12. \u2713\n\nAnd line 9's `day > 31` branch: day=32: \"02-32-2023\": line 9: 2 not in list; 32 > 31 \u2192 True \u2192 return False at line 10. \u2713 Or \"01-32-2023\": (1 in list and 32<1) False; 32>31 True \u2192 False. Either works. Use month=2 to show it's caught at line 9's day>31 arm, and month=1 for the composite arm... Actually for branch coverage of line 9's `or`: branch 1 = `month in [...] and day < 1` being the deciding true, branch 2 = `day > 31` being true. Test \"01-00-2023\" (first arm true) and \"02-32-2023\" (second arm true). Also both false: \"01-15-2023\".\n\nSimilarly line 11: \"04-00-2023\" (first arm), \"04-31-2023\" (second arm, day>30). Both false: \"04-15-2023\".\n\nLine 13: \"02-00-2023\" (first arm), \"02-30-2023\" (second arm, day>29). Both false: \"02-15-2023\" \u2192 True.\n\nLine 7: month<1: \"00-15-2023\"; month>12: \"13-15-2023\".\n\nException paths: \n- unpack ValueError: \"01-2023\" \u2192 split gives 2 elements \u2192 ValueError \u2192 False. \u2713\n- too many: \"01-15-2023-x\" \u2192 ValueError. \u2713\n- non-numeric: \"aa-bb-cccc\" \u2192 int fails \u2192 False. \u2713\n- None: None.strip() \u2192 AttributeError \u2192 False. \u2713\n- wrong separator: \"01/15/2023\" \u2192 split('-') \u2192 1 element \u2192 ValueError. \u2713\n\nAlso, does the function return True/False as booleans? Yes, literal True/False. Assert with `is True` / `is False` to be strict. Hmm, `is` assertions are fine here since returns are literal bools. But maybe safer to use `assert valid_date(...) == True`? `is True` is fine.\n\nOne more consideration: `day < 1` with negative day, e.g., \"01--5-2023\"? split('-') on \"01--5-2023\" gives ['01', '', '5', '2023'] \u2014 4 elements \u2192 ValueError. Not needed.\n\nWhat about negative month, e.g., \"-1-15-2023\" \u2192 split gives ['', '1', '15', '2023'] \u2192 ValueError. Not needed; \"00\" covers month<1.\n\nNow, the tests should be run with pytest. No state pollution \u2014 function is pure, no files. So no monkeypatch needed. But instructions emphasize cleanup; since there's no state, plain tests are fine.\n\nLet me also double check \"12-01-1999\": month=12, day=1. Line 7: ok. Line 9: (12 in list and 1<1) False; 1>31 False. Line 11: 12 not in list; 1>30 False. Line 13: 12 != 2; 1>29 False. \u2192 True. \u2713\n\n\"11-28-2022\": month=11 in [4,6,9,11], day=28. Line 9: 11 not in first list; 28>31 F. Line 11: (11 in list and 28<1) F; 28>30 F. Line 13: 11!=2; 28>29 F \u2192 True. \u2713\n\nFinal test script:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for f124.valid_date covering all lines and branches.\"\"\"\n\n\nfrom f124 import valid_date\n\n\n\ndef test_valid_dates_return_true():\n    \"\"\"Days 1..29 with months 1..12 are accepted; covers the success path (line 18).\"\"\"\n    assert valid_date(\"01-15-2023\") is True\n    assert valid_date(\"02-15-2023\") is True\n    assert valid_date(\"04-15-2023\") is True\n    assert valid_date(\"11-28-2022\") is True\n    assert valid_date(\"12-01-1999\") is True\n\n\n\ndef test_valid_date_strips_surrounding_whitespace():\n    \"\"\"Covers the strip() call (line 4) actually transforming the input.\"\"\"\n    assert valid_date(\"  01-15-2023  \") is True\n    assert valid_date(\"\\t02-29-2020\\n\") is True\n\n\n\ndef test_month_below_lower_bound_returns_false():\n    assert valid_date(\"00-15-2023\") is False\n\n\ndef test_month_above_upper_bound_returns_false():\n    assert valid_date(\"13-15-2023\") is False\n\n\ndef test_31_day_month_with_day_below_one_returns_false():\n    # True arm of 'month in [1,3,5,7,8,10,12] and day < 1' on line 9.\n    assert valid_date(\"01-00-2023\") is False\n    assert valid_date(\"07-00-2023\") is False\n\n\n\ndef test_day_above_31_returns_false():\n    # True arm of 'day > 31' on line 9, reached regardless of the month.\n    assert valid_date(\"02-32-2023\") is False\n    assert valid_date(\"12-99-2023\") is False\n\n\ndef test_30_day_month_with_day_below_one_returns_false():\n    # True arm of 'month in [4,6,9,11] and day < 1' on line 11.\n    assert valid_date(\"04-00-2023\") is False\n    assert valid_date(\"09-00-2023\") is False\n\n\n\ndef test_day_31_rejected_via_30_day_check():\n    # True arm of 'day > 30' on line 11.\n    assert valid_date(\"04-31-2023\") is False\n    assert valid_date(\"06-31-2023\") is False\n\n\n\ndef test_february_with_day_below_one_returns_false():\n    # True arm of 'month == 2 and day < 1' on line 13.\n    assert valid_date(\"02-00-2023\") is False\n\n\ndef test_february_with_day_above_29_returns_false():\n    # True arm of 'day > 29' on line 13.\n    assert valid_date(\"02-30-2023\") is False\n\n\ndef test_february_day_29_is_accepted():\n    assert valid_date(\"02-29-2020\") is True\n\n\n\ndef test_too_few_parts_raises_and_returns_false():\n    # ValueError from unpacking on line 5 -> except branch (lines 15-16).\n    assert valid_date(\"01-2023\") is False\n\n\ndef test_too_many_parts_returns_false():\n    assert valid_date(\"01-15-2023-extra\") is False\n\n\n\ndef test_wrong_separator_returns_false():\n    assert valid_date(\"01/15/2023\") is False\n\n\ndef test_non_numeric_parts_return_false():\n    # ValueError from int() on line 6 -> except branch.\n    assert valid_date(\"aa-bb-cccc\") is False\n    assert valid_date(\"01-bb-2023\") is False\n\n\ndef test_none_input_returns_false():\n    # AttributeError from None.strip() on line 4 -> except branch.\n    assert valid_date(None) is False\n```\n\nWait \u2014 check \"02-29-2020\" again: line 9: 2 not in [1,3,...]; 29 > 31 False. Line 11: 2 not in [4,6,9,11]; 29 > 30 False. Line 13: (2==2 and 29<1) \u2192 False; 29 > 29 \u2192 False. Return True. \u2713\n\nCheck \"07-00-2023\": line 9: 7 in list, 0 < 1 \u2192 True \u2192 return False. \u2713\nCheck \"12-99-2023\": line 9: 12 in list but 99<1 False \u2192 (False); 99 > 31 True \u2192 return False. \u2713\nCheck \"09-00-2023\": line 9: 9 not in first list; 0 > 31 False \u2192 pass. Line 11: 9 in [4,6,9,11], 0 < 1 \u2192 True \u2192 False. \u2713\nCheck \"06-31-2023\": line 9: 6 not in list, 31>31 F. Line 11: (6 in list and 31<1) F; 31>30 T \u2192 False. \u2713\nCheck \"02-30-2023\": line 9: 2 not in list; 30>31 F. Line 11: 2 not in list; 30>30 F. Line 13: (True and 30<1) F; 30>29 T \u2192 False. \u2713\nCheck \"02-00-2023\": line 9: no. Line 11: no. Line 13: (2==2 and 0<1) T \u2192 False. \u2713\nCheck \"01-2023\": split \u2192 ['01','2023'] \u2192 unpack to 3 \u2192 ValueError \u2192 except \u2192 False. \u2713\nCheck \"01-15-2023-extra\": 4 elements \u2192 ValueError. \u2713\nCheck \"01/15/2023\": split('-') \u2192 ['01/15/2023'] \u2192 1 element \u2192 ValueError. \u2713\nCheck \"aa-bb-cccc\": int('aa') \u2192 ValueError. \u2713\nCheck \"01-bb-2023\": int('01')=1, int('bb') \u2192 ValueError. \u2713\nCheck None: None.strip \u2192 AttributeError. \u2713\n\nAll assertions verified against actual behavior (including the precedence bugs). \n\nThe instructions say \"make VERY SURE to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution; use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" Since the function is pure with no side effects, no cleanup is needed. I could add a brief note... no, respond only with code.\n\nAlso \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 just th